{
  "about": "Vehicle exteriors as a pedestrian meets them: oriented boxes in the vehicle frame (x forward, y left, z up, origin on the ground under the middle of the car). A 'slab' is given by the line of its top surface in side view ([x, z] rear point then front point), a thickness below it and a y range. Stiffness (N/m) and damping ratio say how the region gives under a body (in series with the body's own tissue). Regions: bumper, grille (bonnet leading edge), bonnet, cowl, windscreen, a_pillar, roof_header, roof, body.",
  "sources": "Generic shapes, estimated. Bumper and bonnet-leading-edge heights follow the usual grouping of front shapes in pedestrian-protection work (EEVC WG17, Euro NCAP): sedan bumper ~0.5 m and bonnet leading edge ~0.7 m, SUV ~0.7 / ~1.0 m, forward-control van a flat front. Windscreen rake 26-34 degrees from horizontal for cars. Damping ratios are high because fronts dent and glass cracks (pedestrian-car restitution is small); region stiffnesses are estimates chosen so 40 km/h head contacts land in the HIC range of Euro NCAP headform grids (hundreds on the bonnet and glass, thousands on the A-pillars and the cowl); to be calibrated against headform data.",
